#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f7af1dba-6300-56ab-93d2-0f3d1b893dad" :question/math-academy-id "e-21809" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^2+10x$ for $x\\in[0,6),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-6,6).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/de/de535d3df741309e51bab7305aedcb27507b078771f0931d4914e16c88220926.svg)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "1ff70a32-336a-5ff2-bfe8-0df06eec1a7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21810" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/e2/e25e66141569f5324759eb15a1dcf8b143f5018cf35e957f7a212ba3b45b3556.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\nb_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4x$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\nb_n = \\dfrac{8}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi x \\sin(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\nb_n = -\\dfrac{8(-1)^n}{n}=\\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n} \\cdot \\sin(nx).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0288041e-13a5-5541-bbd8-c13988faf24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427748} {:answer/id #uuid "319ba16a-7d74-5bcd-99b2-bc9850751276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-(-1)^{n})}{4\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427749} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9fb755-7be5-5ae8-be1c-cea88ce12977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427750} {:answer/id #uuid "82973565-ede8-565e-a9ef-215aaa0446b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427751} {:answer/id #uuid "d703d305-1fef-58e0-ac9f-aedced6da080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d703d305-1fef-58e0-ac9f-aedced6da080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427752} :answer-field/id #uuid "32a95669-d006-58f5-a342-6964952c273c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a38bbe2-8cc7-5da0-8136-af259e1c0160" :question/math-academy-id "q-340708" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc20d32-5af1-5435-abc5-4668ce0e44f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}-4x+9|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379570} {:answer/id #uuid "75a227c8-8086-59dd-be4b-fc392dab1a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}-4|x|-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379571} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe30399-f024-50fa-99cc-49507726732c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+4x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379572} {:answer/id #uuid "7624e34c-bc9a-5db4-b2f9-1e263ec50973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+4x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379573} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eb5fc9-b49d-5069-afff-7884f7f94646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-4x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7624e34c-bc9a-5db4-b2f9-1e263ec50973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+4x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379573} :answer-field/id #uuid "4211b349-1586-553e-b79d-b35e6c14641e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f55ac59a-df5b-57d9-8289-eba696ad592d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340863" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+4x-9$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+4(-x)-9) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-4x-9) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+4x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5ee3c3f-4289-5e38-a47a-52e5c2ad3817" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Odd Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3422b384-f2f9-572e-8eb4-d8bd9d567439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312396} {:answer/id #uuid "399ebb03-9a48-5ae4-ad4b-e2f9708d9ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312443} {:answer/id #uuid "7b4b355d-f37a-5b71-acc6-9fcb7764f37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312959} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbb155a-24f1-5fdf-9331-fc35b1aa9681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-3{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312966} {:answer/id #uuid "d8724818-0c32-5508-ba36-fc4ff13554d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8724818-0c32-5508-ba36-fc4ff13554d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313679} :answer-field/id #uuid "398ee33f-318a-5b30-b174-de823e5df8f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9a5c0f3-170f-537d-a310-26965c54349f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340866" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,5),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-5,5).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-5,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a85c9c4-8563-55e0-9d4f-7d61d3be6243" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fd64ac7-7e08-59ab-bbc4-4251ea963cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{((-1)^{n}-1)}{8\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379584} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3ac050-5e12-51cf-8cda-18b9be41235f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379585} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5a389f-8a44-5527-a66c-161fc6d48510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379586} {:answer/id #uuid "cba62257-ac04-5987-b049-6fc916e0aceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379587} {:answer/id #uuid "837645a9-f89d-5d26-adaf-7ea72da87792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba62257-ac04-5987-b049-6fc916e0aceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379587} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed63a95-9aef-53fb-825c-118fcd9dacc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3d82ee9-9c73-5ee8-96d1-0448b9926883" :question/math-academy-id "q-340906" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{8((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}.\n$$"}]}
